# Advanced Activities Guide

## 1. Update HTML (`index.html`)

Add this section to your `body`, below the intermediate section:

```html
    <hr>
    <!-- Area for Advanced Activities -->
    <h2>Advanced Styling</h2>
    <div id="box" style="width: 100px; height: 100px; background-color: lightgray; margin-bottom: 10px;">
        I am a box
    </div>
    
    <button class="color-btn" data-color="red">Turn Red</button>
    <button class="color-btn" data-color="blue">Turn Blue</button>
    <button class="color-btn" data-color="green">Turn Green</button>
    <button id="toggleBtn">Toggle Dark Mode</button>

    <style>
        .dark-mode {
            background-color: black;
            color: white;
            border: 2px solid yellow;
        }
    </style>

    <script src="advanced.js"></script>
```

## 2. The JavaScript Code (`advanced.js`)

Create a file named `advanced.js` and add this code:

```javascript
// 1. Create multiple buttons (We did this in HTML)

// 2. Change text color and content dynamically when clicked
let colorButtons = document.querySelectorAll(".color-btn");
let box = document.getElementById("box");

// Loop through all buttons to add listeners
colorButtons.forEach(function(btn) {
    btn.addEventListener("click", function() {
        // Read the 'data-color' attribute (red, blue, green)
        let color = btn.getAttribute("data-color");
        
        // Change the box style
        box.style.backgroundColor = color;
        box.style.color = "white";
        box.innerText = "I am now " + color;
    });
});

// 3. Try combining CSS style changes in JavaScript (Class Toggling)
let toggleBtn = document.getElementById("toggleBtn");

toggleBtn.addEventListener("click", function() {
    // Instead of changing individual styles (style.color = ...),
    // we just add/remove a CSS class. This is much cleaner!
    box.classList.toggle("dark-mode");
});
```

## Explanation & "How To"

### `document.querySelectorAll` vs `getElementById`
**Goal:** Select things.
*   `getElementById("id")`: Gets **one** specific item. Best for unique things.
*   `querySelectorAll(".class")`: Gets **a list** of items. Good for applying the same logic to many buttons.

### Direct Style (`element.style.property`)
**Goal:** Change one specific visual thing.
**Pros:** Good for dynamic values (like a color picker or progress bar width).
**Cons:** Gets messy if you change 10 properties at once.

### Class Toggling (`classList.toggle`)
**Goal:** Switch between "states" or themes.
**Pros:** **Best Practice**. You define the look in CSS (`.dark-mode`), and JS just flips the switch. Keeps JS code simple.
**Cons:** You need to write the CSS beforehand.
